    def order_ideal(self):
        r"""Return ``O_M(v) = {phi(v) : phi in Hom_R(M,R)}``.
    
        This is the image ideal of evaluation at this element, an ideal of
        the scalar ring ``R``: the linear functionals on a vector take values
        in the ring they map into, so on a ring read as a module over its
        base ring this is an ideal of that base ring and not of the ring
        itself.
    
        The ideal is the image of the evaluation morphism, the map
        ``M^* -> R`` that pairs a functional with this element
        (:meth:`dual_evaluation`), computed as that image
        (:meth:`ModuleMorphism.image`) from the values of a generating
        family of the dual.  No basis of the module is read.
    
        The hypothesis of that computation is that the library holds a
        generating family of the dual, and it is asserted (``STY-49``).  The
        algebraic dual of a free module on an infinite basis $S$ is the
        product $\prod_S R$, and the library holds no presentation of it: at
        $S = \mathbf{N}$ and $R = \mathbf{Z}$ it is uncountable, so no
        countable family of its points generates it -- a countable family
        generates a countable submodule -- and by Baer--Specker it is not
        free.  Such a module is answered by the level that owns a basis
        instead, ``FramedFreeModules.ElementMethods.order_ideal``, which
        reads the ideal off the coordinate functionals: every value of a
        functional at $v = \sum_s a_s e_s$ is a combination of the
        coordinates $a_s$, and each coordinate is attained by its own
        functional, so the two ideals are equal.  The rebuild rule
        ``refine._final_category_operations`` orders the two levels by the
        category hierarchy, and the framed level, a subcategory of
        ``Modules(R).Free()``, is the more specific one.
    
        EXAMPLES::
    
            sage: ring = ZZ['x']
            sage: x = ring.algebra_generator('x')
            sage: (2 * x + 2).order_ideal() == ZZ.ideal(2)
            True
        """
        module = self.parent()
        ring = module.base_ring()
        dual = module.dual_module()
